        verification storySprintScaffoldDoD : Test { :>> id = "b0bfd391-200b-4d62-9fb9-eebdb358f06b"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "DELIVERED BACKLOG ITEMS: dcSprintScaffold. Their st013 applied to its largest consumer: every sprint record hand-authored ~14 uuids plus the 7-gate skeleton. DONE AS SPECIFIED: `keel new sprint <N> <slug> --charter <decision> [--points P]` writes the Story + DoD + six ceremony gates with EVERY id minted (checked in tests against guard 38's own predicate, unique per scaffold), createdAt from the real clock (the one authoring field where the clock IS the truth - judgment dates stay refused-not-defaulted), createdBy/owner from the bound actor and REFUSED when absent, the charter verified to be a DECLARED Decision before the edge is written, sprint-number reuse and overwrite refused, and every judgment-bearing text set to the FILL-ME token. THE TOKEN IS ENFORCED: guard 40 (scaffold-placeholder, hard, in GUARD_NAMES with dispatch, doc row, and the CLAUDE.md counts) plus the fast per-edit tier reject any model file carrying it - an unfilled skeleton cannot pass a gate or be committed, by construction. NO TestResults are generated; results are appended when a gate is actually judged. Exercised LIVE on a copy tree (scaffold, fast-gate rejection exit 1, number-reuse/bad-slug/unknown-charter/missing-charter/unbound-actor refusals) and on THIS tree: this record is the command's first real output, held red by its own guard until these texts were filled. 215 unit tests; clippy 0; 40 guards ALL PASS."; }
        part storySprintScaffoldDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "258e0a24-bb85-43e7-a471-1a5b7a59cf89"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "74ecefb"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-21"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
    }

